  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Key Configuration**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the `.env` file and environment variable loading. My analysis indicates that the `loadEnv` function will search for files such as `.env` and `.env.local`. I\'m also examining the scenario where `process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY` is not available, considering potential issues with how the platform passes environment variables to the `vite` process or whether the user has properly connected their configuration.\n\n\n**Analyzing API Key Handling**\n\nI\'ve shifted my attention to how the API key is handled. I noticed a discrepancy in the screenshot; it appears the key\'s value might be coming from a selection, not a direct entry. I\'m going to make the `gemini.ts` file a bit more communicative with console logs to give the user better insights. Additionally, I\'ll revise `vite.config.ts`, considering how empty strings are handled in environment variable replacements to prevent potential conflicts.\n\n\n**Refining API Key Retrieval**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring the API key is correctly retrieved and available. I will use `VITE_GEMINI_API_KEY` as the primary key name, mapping `GEMINI_API_KEY` to it if necessary. I\'m also adding console logs in `vite.config.ts` to confirm its existence. I will ensure that the environment variables are being injected correctly, and consider whether the setup\'s timing could be a factor. I\'ll double-check `dotenv` usage as well. My past results indicated the key check failed, so I\'m investigating how that happened.\n\n\n**Addressing Key Injection Issue**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the central issue: the secret key isn\'t present in `process.env` during build or restart. This renders the `apiKey` falsy in `vite.config.ts`, and subsequently, `process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY` is also falsy during that phase. I realized AI Studio injects secrets into the environment. So, I will try restarting the dev server with modifications to manage a possible alternative environment variable name. I also considered, and discarded, directly accessing `process.env` in `gemini.ts`. Now, I\'m leaning toward a server-side proxy endpoint, which is apparently the recommended secure approach.\n\n\n**Implementing Server-Side Proxy**\n\nI\'m now implementing the full-stack pattern as the primary and recommended solution. First, I\'ll modify `package.json` to include a server script and create `server.ts` with Express and Vite middleware. I\'ll then create a proxy route in `server.ts` for Gemini, and update `gemini.ts` to call my internal API instead of the SDK client-side. This ensures the API key remains protected on the server. I will then restart the dev server to test. I need to make sure the app works and that the key is accessible on the backend. This is a larger architectural shift, but it ensures secret key protection.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
